From 1aaa6883572740628dc14f0b11c2e21801d3aeba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:21:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: a v4.0 part that cannot be carried no longer takes
 native Dolby Vision away

A block of the content mapping v4.0 part that the conversion for the tunnel
does not take (a length or a value that dovi_tool does not know, padding
that is not 0, a block of more than 32 bytes whose bytes FFmpeg does not
keep), or a payload that would be over the 512 bytes of ETSI GS CCM 001 with
that part, refused the whole frame, with a line on stderr only. 24 refused
frames in 2 s take native output away, so such a title lost native Dolby
Vision for good. dovi_tool's largest metadata (eight level 2, five level 8,
four level 10, and levels 3, 4, 9, 11 and 254) is 583 bytes on the wire.

Now the v4.0 part (levels 3, 8 to 11 and 254) is taken out of that frame and
left out for the rest of the stream, as it is for a display of display
management 2 or 3. The v2.9 part (levels 1, 2, 4 and 5; what CCM 001
defines) is never touched: with dovi_tool's blocks it is 256 bytes at most.
It stays out until the stream ends (dvbridge_dv_restart()), so that a
display does not switch between the two mappings from scene to scene, and a
frame that is not presented changes nothing (the state advances with a
commit only). A level 4 block that cannot be carried still refuses the
frame. The room of a level 5 block made for the raster is kept, so every
payload that was sent before is sent byte for byte the same: the serializer
host test compares the payload of each of its 250 frames with the bridge
before, and only the frames that it refused differ.

kodi.log says it once a stream, with the reason and the block (its level
and its length); the first-picture line and the Dolby Vision page of the
player information say "v2.9 (its v4.0 part cannot be carried)" (skin
string 32053, with its es_es, es_ar and es_mx translations).
